/* generated by build.py - do not edit */
window.BOOK=[{"id":1,"title":"What is a SOC?","part":"Foundations","summary":"The function, the structure and the daily reality of a security operations centre.","sections":[{"h":"Definition","p":"A Security Operations Centre is the function responsible for detecting, investigating and responding to security events across an organisation. It is a capability rather than a room: people, process and tooling combined so that suspicious activity is noticed, understood and acted on continuously."},{"h":"What it actually does","p":"Monitoring telemetry from endpoints, servers, network devices, identity platforms and cloud services. Triaging detections into real, benign or needs-more-work. Investigating the real ones. Containing what is confirmed. Feeding everything learned back into detection content."},{"h":"How work arrives","p":"Almost all work arrives as a queue of detections raised by correlation rules, EDR behavioural analytics, mail security, DLP and user reports. A mature SOC also generates work proactively through threat hunting, where the analyst starts from a hypothesis rather than an alert."},{"h":"The shift model","p":"Coverage is usually 24/7 through follow-the-sun or rotating shifts. Handover between shifts is a formal process: open cases, active escalations, pending actions and anything a colleague must chase. Handover failures are one of the most common causes of long detection-to-action gaps."},{"h":"What good looks like","p":"Short time to triage, short time to contain, a false positive rate low enough that analysts have attention left for real work, detection coverage measured against a framework, and every escalation with a named owner. Volume of alerts closed is not a quality measure."},{"h":"Common misconception","p":"A SOC is not a wall that stops attacks. It is the function that finds what got past the walls, reduces the damage, and makes the walls better next time. Measuring a SOC by whether incidents happen is measuring the wrong thing; measure it by how quickly they are found and how far they get."}]},{"id":2,"title":"SOC Analyst Roles","part":"Foundations","summary":"Who does what in a SOC, and how the roles interact during an incident.","sections":[{"h":"Analyst roles","p":"Tier 1 triages the queue and performs initial investigation. Tier 2 investigates escalations in depth and drives containment. Tier 3 handles the hardest cases, threat hunting and forensics. Alongside them sit detection engineers who build and tune rules, threat intelligence analysts who track actors, and incident managers who coordinate response and communication."},{"h":"The incident manager","p":"Owns the incident rather than the analysis: sequencing, stakeholder communication, legal and regulatory engagement, and the decision to invoke crisis process. During a major incident the analyst supplies facts and recommendations; the incident manager decides and records."},{"h":"Adjacent functions","p":"Infrastructure and platform teams execute most containment actions (firewall changes, account disabling, host isolation). Identity teams own credential resets. Legal and privacy own breach notification. The SOC rarely has unilateral authority over production, which is why precise, well-evidenced recommendations matter more than access."},{"h":"Working with intelligence","p":"Threat intelligence should change decisions. If an actor profile says exfiltration precedes extortion within 72 hours, that changes the urgency of containment. Intelligence that only decorates a report is wasted effort."},{"h":"Career shape","p":"Most analysts enter at Tier 1 and move toward one of three directions: deeper investigation and forensics, detection engineering, or incident management. All three benefit from the same foundation - the ability to read raw telemetry and reason from evidence."}]},{"id":3,"title":"Tier 1 / Tier 2 / Tier 3","part":"Foundations","summary":"What each tier is accountable for, and how to escalate well.","sections":[{"h":"Tier 1 accountability","p":"Work the queue in risk order, determine true or false positive with evidence, contain obvious cases within delegated authority, escalate anything touching critical assets or exceeding delegated scope, and document everything so the next person can continue without asking."},{"h":"Tier 2 accountability","p":"Deep investigation across multiple data sources, scoping (how many hosts, how many identities), attribution support, containment planning, and the technical narrative that goes into the incident report."},{"h":"Tier 3 accountability","p":"Forensics, malware analysis, threat hunting, complex correlation, and the detection content that comes out of an incident. Tier 3 also validates whether detections actually fire, usually through purple team exercises."},{"h":"How to escalate well","p":"State what you observed, what you concluded, what evidence supports it, what you have already done, and what you need. An escalation that says 'this looks bad, please look' transfers work without transferring understanding."},{"h":"When to escalate immediately","p":"Any confirmed activity on a Tier-0 asset, any evidence of domain-level credential compromise, any confirmed data movement, any impact-stage activity such as shadow copy deletion or backup tampering. These do not wait for the end of your analysis."},{"h":"Common mistake","p":"Escalating everything, or escalating nothing. Both are failures of judgement. The measure is whether a Tier-2 analyst receiving your escalation would agree it needed them."}]},{"id":4,"title":"Alert Triage","part":"Core Skills","summary":"The decision layer between detection and investigation.","sections":[{"h":"The three questions","p":"Is the observed behaviour real? Is it authorised? Does it need escalation? Every triage decision is an answer to those three, supported by a named artefact."},{"h":"Ranking the queue","p":"Rank by risk, not by arrival order or by the severity label the tool printed. Risk combines the severity of the behaviour with the criticality of the asset and the identity involved. A MEDIUM detection on a domain controller outranks a HIGH on a lab VM."},{"h":"Closing noise correctly","p":"A false positive closure must name the evidence: a change ticket, a deployment job, a signed binary from an expected path, a documented business process. 'Looked fine' is not a closure."},{"h":"Patterns that mean automation","p":"Byte-identical command lines across many hosts within minutes, a signed management agent as the parent process, and no outbound network activity almost always mean software deployment, not intrusion."},{"h":"Patterns that mean intrusion","p":"Unsigned binaries in user-writable paths, execution chains that begin with a user document or shortcut, authentication from infrastructure with no business relationship, and behaviour that continues after the initial event."},{"h":"Documenting a decision","p":"Record the alert ID, the verdict, the evidence, the action taken and any tuning request. The tuning request is what stops the same alert consuming the next shift too."},{"h":"Common mistake","p":"Triaging alerts individually when they are one campaign. Before closing, check whether the same source, account or host appears elsewhere in the queue."}]},{"id":5,"title":"Log Analysis","part":"Core Skills","summary":"Reading raw telemetry without a dashboard in front of you.","sections":[{"h":"Why raw matters","p":"Dashboards normalise and therefore lose detail. Field names differ between vendors; the underlying event does not. An analyst who can read raw Windows events, syslog, firewall logs and flow records can work anywhere."},{"h":"The five questions per line","p":"When, where, who, what and from where. Timestamp, host, account, action, source address. If a log line does not answer those, find the source that does."},{"h":"Establishing a baseline","p":"Suspicion requires a norm. Before calling something anomalous, know what the account, host or service does on an ordinary day - working hours, source addresses, typical volume."},{"h":"Timezones","p":"Normalise everything to UTC and state the timezone in every finding. Mixed timezones produce sequences that look causal but are not, and that error survives into reports."},{"h":"Counting","p":"Counts are evidence. Twelve failed logons is a finding; 'several failures' is an impression. Count precisely and state the window the count covers."},{"h":"Common mistake","p":"Reading only the alerting event. The events immediately before and after it usually carry the actual story - especially anything that happens after a successful authentication."}]},{"id":6,"title":"SIEM","part":"Core Skills","summary":"Correlation, search and the analyst workflow inside a SIEM.","sections":[{"h":"What a SIEM does","p":"Collects logs from many sources, normalises them into common fields, retains them for search, and runs correlation rules that raise detections when patterns match."},{"h":"The core workflow","p":"Scope a time window. Filter to relevant sources. Pivot on an entity - an IP, user, host or hash. Aggregate to find outliers. Correlate across sources to build the sequence. Repeat with each new entity discovered."},{"h":"Pivoting","p":"Pivoting is the skill that separates search from investigation. One confirmed indicator searched across every index produces more than ten alert reviews, because each hit generates the next search term."},{"h":"Aggregation","p":"Counts by source, distinct destinations per account, events per minute. Outliers surface through aggregation far faster than through reading. 'Distinct hosts authenticated to per account per hour' catches lateral movement that no single event reveals."},{"h":"Correlation rules","p":"Rules join events across sources - for example, a failed logon burst followed by a success from the same address, followed by process creation on the target. Poorly written joins produce spurious matches, so always verify the underlying events."},{"h":"Evidence discipline","p":"Record every query with its time window. A count without a window cannot be reproduced, and an unreproducible number does not belong in a report."}]},{"id":7,"title":"EDR","part":"Core Skills","summary":"Endpoint detection and response as an investigation tool.","sections":[{"h":"What EDR records","p":"Process creation with full command lines and parent relationships, file writes, registry modifications, module loads, network connections and in many products memory events - all linked into a process tree."},{"h":"The process tree","p":"The tree is the investigation. Work outward from the alerting process: what started it, what it started, what it wrote, what it contacted. Most endpoint cases resolve entirely within the tree."},{"h":"Signals that matter","p":"Unsigned binaries, execution from user-writable paths, impossible parent-child relationships, system binary names running from the wrong directory, and processes making outbound connections to raw IP addresses."},{"h":"Response actions","p":"Network isolation (the host stays up for investigation but cannot reach anything except the console), process termination, file quarantine, and blocking by hash or path across the estate. Isolation preserves evidence; shutdown destroys memory."},{"h":"Limitations","p":"Coverage is rarely complete - Linux, network appliances, OT and unmanaged devices are often blind spots. An intrusion that begins where EDR is not installed will be invisible until it reaches a covered host."},{"h":"Common mistake","p":"Trusting the product's verdict instead of reading the tree. EDR severity is an input to your judgement, not a replacement for it."}]},{"id":8,"title":"Network Monitoring","part":"Core Skills","summary":"Reading behaviour from traffic when you cannot read content.","sections":[{"h":"Data sources","p":"Flow records (NetFlow, IPFIX), firewall session logs, proxy logs, DNS resolver logs, TLS metadata and Zeek-style connection summaries. Full packet capture exists in some environments but is rarely available retrospectively."},{"h":"Shape over content","p":"Encryption hides payload but not behaviour. How often, how long, how much, in which direction, and to where - these five properties identify beaconing, exfiltration, scanning and tunnelling without decrypting anything."},{"h":"Beaconing","p":"Regular intervals with small, symmetric payloads. Jitter is added deliberately to defeat visual inspection, so average the intervals rather than eyeballing them. A ten-minute cadence that matches a scheduled task on the host is conclusive."},{"h":"Volume asymmetry","p":"Far more out than in suggests exfiltration; far more in than out suggests tool download. Neither is conclusive alone - backup replication and software updates produce both patterns legitimately."},{"h":"TLS metadata","p":"Certificate details (self-signed, CN=localhost, recently issued), SNI, and client fingerprints such as JA3 identify tooling even when the content is opaque. A fingerprint shared across unrelated destinations often reveals the same operator."},{"h":"Internal traffic","p":"A compromised host with no internet route is still being controlled. Look for internal pivots: SMB named pipes, internal HTTP proxies, or a relay host that has both internal reach and egress."}]},{"id":9,"title":"Windows Security","part":"Platforms","summary":"The event IDs and artefacts that matter most on Windows.","sections":[{"h":"Authentication events","p":"4624 successful logon, 4625 failed logon, 4634 logoff, 4648 logon with explicit credentials, 4672 special privileges assigned, 4740 account lockout. Logon types: 2 interactive, 3 network, 4 batch, 5 service, 10 RemoteInteractive (RDP)."},{"h":"Sub-status codes","p":"On 4625, 0xC000006A means the account exists but the password was wrong (password guessing) and 0xC0000064 means the account does not exist (username enumeration). 0xC000015B means the logon type was not granted - usually a segmentation or policy control working as intended."},{"h":"Process and script events","p":"4688 process creation with parent and command line; 4104 PowerShell script block logging, which records code after de-obfuscation and is the single most valuable execution artefact on Windows."},{"h":"Directory change events","p":"4720 account created, 4722 enabled, 4724 password reset, 4726 deleted, 4728 added to a global group, 4732 added to a local group, 4738 account changed. The Subject field is who did it; the Target field is to whom."},{"h":"Service and share events","p":"7045 service installed, 5140 and 5145 network share access. A 7045 with an unsigned binary outside System32 and Program Files is one of the highest-fidelity indicators available."},{"h":"Log integrity","p":"1102 records that the security log was cleared. An absence of records is itself evidence, and volume shadow copies frequently preserve what was cleared."}]},{"id":10,"title":"Linux Security","part":"Platforms","summary":"Authentication, execution and persistence evidence on Linux systems.","sections":[{"h":"Where the evidence lives","p":"/var/log/auth.log or /var/log/secure for SSH and sudo, the systemd journal for service events, auditd where deployed, shell history files, cron tables, systemd units and ~/.ssh/authorized_keys."},{"h":"SSH patterns","p":"'Failed password for invalid user X' means the account does not exist - enumeration. 'Failed password for root' means it does. 'Accepted password' on a host that should be key-only is itself a finding. Sequential source ports across attempts indicate an automated tool."},{"h":"Privilege use","p":"sudo entries record TTY, working directory, target user and the exact COMMAND. Reading sudo history after a suspicious login is usually the fastest route to understanding what the intruder did."},{"h":"Persistence surfaces","p":"cron and systemd timers, systemd unit files, rc scripts, shell profiles, SUID binaries, and additional entries in authorized_keys. A new key with an innocuous comment is a common and easily missed backdoor."},{"h":"Anti-forensics","p":"'history -c' clears the in-memory buffer but does not remove what has already been written to the history file, and it does not touch auth.log or the journal. Hidden files in /tmp and /dev/shm are a standard habit worth checking explicitly."},{"h":"Coverage reality","p":"Linux telemetry is frequently weaker than Windows in enterprise estates. Where auditd or EDR coverage is partial, assume investigation will depend on text logs and filesystem timestamps, and raise the gap as a finding."}]},{"id":11,"title":"Active Directory","part":"Platforms","summary":"Why the directory is the centre of most enterprise intrusions.","sections":[{"h":"What AD is","p":"The identity and authorisation database of a Windows estate: users, computers, groups, policies and trusts. Control of the directory is effectively control of everything that authenticates against it."},{"h":"Tiering","p":"A tiered model separates Tier 0 (domain controllers, identity infrastructure), Tier 1 (servers and applications) and Tier 2 (workstations), and forbids credentials from a higher tier being used on a lower one. Tier violations are how a workstation compromise becomes a domain compromise."},{"h":"High-value objects","p":"Domain Admins, Enterprise Admins, Backup Operators, Account Operators, the krbtgt account, GPOs linked to many objects, and any service account with an SPN and an old password."},{"h":"Attack surface","p":"Kerberoasting (service tickets for offline cracking), AS-REP roasting (accounts without pre-authentication), DCSync (replication rights abused to pull hashes), golden and silver tickets (forged Kerberos tickets), GPO abuse to run code estate-wide, and ACL abuse to grant rights quietly."},{"h":"What to monitor","p":"Group membership changes on privileged groups, account creation outside change control, 4769 bursts with RC4 encryption, DS-Replication-Get-Changes-All by non-DC accounts, and GPO modifications."},{"h":"After a domain compromise","p":"If DCSync or a DC compromise is confirmed, every credential in the domain is suspect. krbtgt must be reset twice with a replication cycle between resets, and recovery planning should assume a rebuild of trust rather than a cleanup."}]},{"id":12,"title":"Authentication Attacks","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Brute force, spraying, stuffing, and abuse of valid credentials.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"Attacks that obtain or abuse credentials to authenticate as a legitimate identity, rather than exploiting a software vulnerability."},{"h":"Why attackers use it","p":"Valid credentials generate no exploit artefacts, survive patching, and grant exactly the access the legitimate user has. It is the cheapest and most reliable route into most enterprises."},{"h":"Variants","p":"Brute force (many passwords, one account), password spraying (one common password, many accounts, staying under lockout thresholds), credential stuffing (username/password pairs stolen elsewhere), and use of credentials bought from infostealer marketplaces."},{"h":"What it looks like in logs","p":"Windows: 4625 bursts, then a 4624 from the same source. The sub-status separates password guessing from username enumeration. Linux: repeated 'Failed password' then 'Accepted password'. Cloud: sign-ins from unfamiliar ASNs, impossible travel, repeated MFA prompts."},{"h":"How to investigate","p":"Identify the source address and account, count the failures precisely, find the first and last suspicious events, determine whether authentication succeeded, then examine everything the account did afterwards - which is where the intrusion actually begins."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Block the source, reset the credential, revoke sessions and tokens (a reset alone leaves an active session working), remove any attacker-registered MFA method, and check for accounts created or promoted while the credential was live."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1110 Brute Force, T1110.003 Password Spraying, T1078 Valid Accounts, T1621 MFA Request Generation."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Resetting the password but not revoking sessions; missing the backdoor account created during the window; treating an internal user's two failed attempts as an attack."}]},{"id":13,"title":"Phishing","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Email as the dominant initial access vector.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"Messages crafted to make a recipient execute a file, click a credential-harvesting link, or perform an action such as changing payment details."},{"h":"Why attackers use it","p":"It scales, requires no vulnerability, and targets the one component that cannot be patched. Targeted variants impersonate real partners and reference real business processes."},{"h":"What it looks like","p":"Look-alike sender domains, authentication failures (SPF, DKIM, DMARC), Received hops through unrelated infrastructure, urgency and deadlines in the body, and attachments that execute: .lnk, .iso, .img, .html, macro-enabled documents, and password-protected archives whose password is in the message."},{"h":"Why encrypted archives matter","p":"A gateway cannot inspect an encrypted archive. Supplying the password in the body defeats content scanning while remaining trivially usable by the victim. Treat that combination as hostile until proven otherwise."},{"h":"How to investigate","p":"Analyse headers first, then the payload, then scope: how many recipients, how many opened, how many extracted, how many executed. The execution count defines the incident; the delivery count defines the campaign."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Purge from all mailboxes, block the sender domain and its supporting infrastructure, isolate endpoints where execution occurred, reset affected credentials, and issue targeted awareness that names the specific lure."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1566.001 Spearphishing Attachment, T1566.002 Spearphishing Link, T1204.002 User Execution."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Treating the reporting user as the problem. Users who report are the control that worked; reinforce it publicly."}]},{"id":14,"title":"Malware","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Loaders, implants and how to triage a sample from a report.","sections":[{"h":"Categories","p":"Loaders and droppers (establish a foothold and fetch more), remote access implants, infostealers, ransomware encryptors, and living-off-the-land tooling that uses legitimate signed binaries instead of custom code."},{"h":"Static triage","p":"Hashes for identification, imports for capability, strings for infrastructure and behaviour, entropy and section names for packing, compile timestamp and signature for provenance."},{"h":"Dynamic triage","p":"Sandbox execution recording process, file, registry and network behaviour: mutexes created, persistence written, domains resolved, beacon intervals, injection targets."},{"h":"Capability from imports","p":"VirtualAllocEx with WriteProcessMemory and CreateRemoteThread indicates process injection. CryptEncrypt suggests ransomware. InternetOpenUrl indicates download capability. Capability inferred from imports is more reliable than a family name."},{"h":"Indicator durability","p":"File hashes change with every rebuild and are the weakest indicator. Mutex names, URI paths, user-agents, registry value names and behavioural sequences are baked into the tooling and survive recompilation."},{"h":"What to do with the report","p":"Convert every artefact into a hunt query and a detection candidate, then sweep the estate. A triage report that produces no detections has produced nothing."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1027 Obfuscated Files, T1055 Process Injection, T1204 User Execution, T1105 Ingress Tool Transfer."}]},{"id":15,"title":"Persistence","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"How attackers guarantee they can come back.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"Any mechanism that returns execution to the attacker after a reboot, a logoff or a cleanup attempt."},{"h":"Why attackers use it","p":"Access obtained is access that can be lost. Persistence protects the investment, and experienced operators plant several mechanisms of different types expecting the obvious one to be found."},{"h":"Windows mechanisms","p":"Run and RunOnce registry values, scheduled tasks, services (7045), WMI event filter and consumer pairs, startup folder shortcuts, DLL search-order hijacking, image file execution options, and accounts added to privileged groups."},{"h":"Linux mechanisms","p":"cron entries and systemd timers, systemd units, rc scripts, shell profile modifications, SUID binaries, and additional SSH authorized_keys entries."},{"h":"How to hunt it","p":"Enumerate by creation time rather than by suspicion. Everything created during the confirmed attack window deserves inspection. Check WMI subscriptions explicitly - they do not appear in ordinary startup locations."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Remove every mechanism in one coordinated action across all hosts, preserve copies first, then verify with a second sweep. Cleaning one host while others still beacon simply invites redeployment."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1547.001 Registry Run Keys, T1053.005 Scheduled Task, T1543.003 Windows Service, T1546.003 WMI Event Subscription, T1098.004 SSH Authorized Keys."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Finding one mechanism and stopping. Deleting rather than preserving. Missing a scheduled task that re-downloads the payload after the payload itself is removed."}]},{"id":16,"title":"Privilege Escalation","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Moving from the rights you have to the rights you want.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"Obtaining higher privileges on a host or in the directory than the compromised identity originally held."},{"h":"Why attackers use it","p":"Before escalation, damage is bounded by one user's access. After it, the attacker can read memory, install services, disable defences and move laterally with stolen credentials."},{"h":"Windows routes","p":"Weak service or directory permissions allowing binary replacement, unquoted service paths, credential theft from LSASS, token impersonation, vulnerable drivers, and simply adding an account to a privileged group when directory write access exists."},{"h":"Linux routes","p":"sudo misconfiguration, SUID binaries, writable systemd units or cron files, and kernel exploits."},{"h":"What it looks like","p":"A process whose integrity level rises without a corresponding UAC prompt, service binaries or configurations modified by non-admin accounts, 4672 for accounts that should never receive special privileges, and group membership changes outside change control."},{"h":"How to investigate","p":"Compare integrity or privilege state before and after on the same code, identify the specific misconfiguration exploited, and establish what the escalated context then did - usually credential access."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Restore the original binary and correct permissions, treat every credential cached on the host as stolen, and audit privileged group membership across the estate."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1068 Exploitation for Privilege Escalation, T1574.011 Services Registry Permissions Weakness, T1134 Access Token Manipulation, T1078 Valid Accounts."}]},{"id":17,"title":"Lateral Movement","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Authenticated movement between hosts using stolen credentials.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"Using credentials obtained on one host to access another, repeated until the attacker reaches the systems they actually want."},{"h":"Why attackers use it","p":"Initial access rarely lands on the valuable system. Movement converts a foothold into reach - and because it uses valid credentials, it looks like administration."},{"h":"Common methods","p":"Remote service creation (PsExec: 4648, Type 3 logon, ADMIN$ access, 7045 PSEXESVC), WMI remote execution, WinRM, RDP (Type 10), remote scheduled tasks, and pass-the-hash producing NTLM network logons."},{"h":"The four-event shape","p":"Most SMB-based movement produces the same sequence: explicit credentials supplied, a network logon on the destination, administrative share access, and a new service. Recognising that shape lets you count movements quickly."},{"h":"How to investigate","p":"Build a movement graph: source host, destination host, account, method, timestamp. Count distinct destinations per account per hour - humans, scripts and management tooling have very different shapes."},{"h":"What blocking looks like","p":"A destination returning 0xC000015B is refusing the logon type, typically because segmentation or a tiered admin model denies network logon to that account. That is a control working, and it belongs in the report."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Disable the account used for movement, isolate affected hosts simultaneously, and assume credentials cached on every visited host are compromised."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1021.002 SMB/Windows Admin Shares, T1021.001 Remote Desktop, T1569.002 Service Execution, T1047 WMI."}]},{"id":18,"title":"Command & Control","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"The channel between implant and operator.","sections":[{"h":"What it is","p":"The communication path an implant uses to receive instructions and return results."},{"h":"Why it matters","p":"C2 is the one stage an attacker cannot skip on a persistent intrusion. Mapping every channel is what makes containment effective; blocking one address rarely ends anything."},{"h":"Transports","p":"HTTP and HTTPS to attacker infrastructure or through a redirector, DNS (queries and TXT answers), legitimate cloud and collaboration services used as dead drops, SMB named pipes for internal pivots, and occasionally ICMP or other protocols."},{"h":"Timing","p":"Fixed intervals with jitter are standard; long-sleep implants that wake hourly or daily exist specifically to survive containment of the primary channel. Average the intervals rather than judging by eye."},{"h":"Fingerprints","p":"TLS client fingerprints (JA3), certificate anomalies (self-signed, CN=localhost, freshly issued), hardcoded user-agents and consistent URI paths identify tooling across rotating infrastructure."},{"h":"Internal pivots","p":"Hosts with no internet route are controlled through a relay - typically an SMB named pipe to a peer that does have egress. If a compromised host cannot reach the internet, find what it is talking to instead."},{"h":"Containment","p":"Block every channel simultaneously: external addresses and domains, DNS sinkholing, and isolation of the relay host. Sequential blocking warns a human operator to adapt."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1071.001 Web Protocols, T1071.004 DNS, T1090.001 Internal Proxy, T1573 Encrypted Channel, T1102 Web Service."}]},{"id":19,"title":"Data Exfiltration","part":"Attack Techniques","summary":"Collection, staging and transfer of stolen data.","sections":[{"h":"The three phases","p":"Collection (finding and reading the data), staging (compressing and often encrypting it into archives, usually in a temp or public directory), and transfer (over C2, DNS, cloud storage, email or removable media)."},{"h":"Why phases matter","p":"Each phase leaves different evidence. The staging archive is usually the best single artefact for quantifying impact, because it states exactly what was collected."},{"h":"Transfer channels","p":"The C2 channel itself for bulk data, DNS tunnelling for low-bandwidth or covert movement, commercial cloud storage via tools such as rclone, webmail, and physical media. Cloud storage is favoured because the destination is a legitimate service that most egress policies allow."},{"h":"Quantifying volume","p":"Convert what you observe into bytes and compare it against what was staged. DNS tunnels carry very little per query, so a large gap between staged volume and tunnel capacity proves another channel exists."},{"h":"What to record","p":"File counts, data classification, record counts and the specific shares or datasets accessed - and also what was not accessible. Legal and privacy teams need scope, not traffic descriptions."},{"h":"Anti-forensics","p":"Archives are commonly deleted after transfer. Volume shadow copies, USN journal entries and prefetch frequently defeat this."},{"h":"MITRE","p":"T1074.001 Local Data Staging, T1560.001 Archive via Utility, T1048.003 Exfiltration Over Alternative Protocol, T1567.002 Exfiltration to Cloud Storage."}]},{"id":20,"title":"Threat Intelligence","part":"Analysis","summary":"Turning knowledge about adversaries into better decisions.","sections":[{"h":"Three layers","p":"Tactical intelligence is indicators - short-lived and immediately actionable. Operational intelligence is tradecraft: tools, techniques and sequencing. Strategic intelligence is targeting, motive and trend."},{"h":"Attribution","p":"A confidence judgement, never a certainty. It rests on overlap across multiple independent axes: infrastructure, tooling artefacts, tradecraft and targeting. A single shared IP proves nothing; hosting is shared."},{"h":"Expressing confidence","p":"State low, moderate or high confidence, the evidence supporting it, and what would change your assessment. Analysts who never express uncertainty are eventually wrong in public."},{"h":"Making it actionable","p":"Intelligence must change a decision. If the actor's profile says exfiltration precedes extortion within 72 hours, containment urgency changes. If it does not change anything, it is decoration."},{"h":"Naming","p":"The same group carries different names across vendors. Track the behaviour and the artefacts; treat the name as a label for correlation, not as knowledge in itself."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Using intelligence to skip evidence collection, over-attributing on commodity tooling, or letting a compelling actor narrative drive conclusions the logs do not support."}]},{"id":21,"title":"IOC Analysis","part":"Analysis","summary":"Extracting, validating and using indicators without drowning in them.","sections":[{"h":"What an indicator is","p":"An artefact observed on a network or host that, with context, suggests compromise: an address, a domain, a hash, a filename, a registry path, a mutex, a certificate thumbprint, a user-agent string. An indicator without context is a string; an indicator with context is evidence."},{"h":"The pyramid of pain","p":"Hashes are trivial for an adversary to change, addresses and domains slightly less so, host and network artefacts harder, tools harder again, and tactics hardest of all. Detections built at the top of the pyramid survive one recompile. Detections built on behaviour survive the campaign."},{"h":"Extraction discipline","p":"Pull indicators from primary evidence only - the raw log line, the EDR process record, the mail header - and record where each came from. An indicator copied from a colleague's note without a source cannot be defended in a report."},{"h":"Validation before blocking","p":"Check whether the address belongs to a CDN, a cloud provider or your own egress NAT before you block it. Blocking a shared hosting address can take out a business service and teach the organisation not to trust the SOC."},{"h":"Pivoting","p":"One confirmed indicator is a starting point, not an answer. Pivot: which other hosts resolved that domain, which other processes wrote that hash, which other accounts authenticated from that address. Blast radius is found by pivoting, not by staring."},{"h":"Ageing and expiry","p":"Indicators decay. A command-and-control address may be reassigned to an unrelated tenant within weeks. Every block and every watchlist entry needs a review date, otherwise the list becomes an unmaintainable source of false positives."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Blocking indicators before understanding scope, which tips the adversary off; treating any hit on a large indicator feed as an incident; and building a report out of indicators alone, with no narrative of what actually happened."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"A beaconing host yields a domain. Passive DNS shows the domain resolved to an address that three other internal hosts also contacted. Those three hosts were not alerting. The pivot - not the original alert - defined the incident."}]},{"id":22,"title":"MITRE ATT&CK","part":"Analysis","summary":"A shared vocabulary for adversary behaviour, and how to use it honestly.","sections":[{"h":"Structure","p":"Tactics are the adversary's goals - initial access, execution, persistence, privilege escalation, defence evasion, credential access, discovery, lateral movement, collection, command and control, exfiltration, impact. Techniques are the ways each goal is met, and sub-techniques are the specific variants."},{"h":"Why it matters to a SOC","p":"It gives analysts, detection engineers, red teams and management one vocabulary. 'Suspicious PowerShell' means different things to different people; T1059.001 does not. It also makes coverage measurable: which techniques can we detect, which can we only detect after the fact, and which are blind."},{"h":"Mapping an incident","p":"Map observed behaviour, not assumed behaviour. Every technique in your map should point at a specific piece of evidence. If you cannot name the log line, the technique does not belong in the report."},{"h":"Common confusions","p":"Valid Accounts (T1078) is used at several stages and analysts often record it once and move on. Remote Services (T1021) is lateral movement, not execution. Scheduled Task (T1053) can be persistence, execution or privilege escalation depending on context."},{"h":"Coverage work","p":"Building a coverage heatmap is useful only when honest. Mark a technique as covered when a detection exists, has fired on a true positive or a purple-team test, and is tuned. Marking it covered because a log source theoretically contains the data is self-deception."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Decorating a report with technique IDs to look sophisticated, mapping to the most exotic technique rather than the plain one, and treating the matrix as a checklist an adversary must follow in order."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"An intrusion maps to T1566.002 for the phishing link, T1078 for the stolen credentials, T1059.001 for the loader, T1053.005 for persistence, T1558.003 for kerberoasting, T1021.002 for lateral movement, T1071.001 for command and control and T1567 for exfiltration - and each of those IDs has a log reference beside it."}]},{"id":23,"title":"Incident Response","part":"Response","summary":"What happens when triage becomes an incident, and the analyst's place in it.","sections":[{"h":"The lifecycle","p":"Preparation, detection and analysis, containment, eradication, recovery, and lessons learned. The phases overlap in practice, but skipping one always costs more later - particularly preparation and lessons learned, which are the two most often abandoned."},{"h":"Declaring an incident","p":"Declaration is a decision with consequences: it calls people in, starts a clock and may trigger legal and regulatory obligations. Declare on defined criteria, not on mood. Every hour of hesitation is an hour of adversary dwell time."},{"h":"Containment sequencing","p":"Contain in an order that does not destroy evidence or tip the adversary prematurely. Capture volatile memory before isolating where feasible. Isolate rather than power off. Reset credentials only when you can reset all of the compromised ones at once - a partial reset just tells the adversary you are watching."},{"h":"Eradication and recovery","p":"Eradication removes access: implants, persistence, credentials, rogue accounts, malicious rules. Recovery restores service with monitoring in place and explicit acceptance criteria. Restoring a host without removing persistence is a very expensive way to do nothing."},{"h":"Roles","p":"An incident commander decides and owns the timeline. Analysts investigate. A scribe records. Communications handles internal and external messaging. Legal advises on obligation. When an analyst is simultaneously investigating and briefing executives, both jobs are done badly."},{"h":"Evidence and records","p":"Timestamps in UTC, an unbroken chain of custody for images and exports, and decisions logged with their rationale. A response that cannot be reconstructed afterwards cannot be defended to a regulator, an insurer or a court."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Rebuilding the first infected host immediately, resetting one account of many, communicating over infrastructure the adversary controls, and closing the incident before persistence has been searched for across the estate."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"A confirmed intrusion on a file server: isolate at the switch, preserve memory and disk, reset every account seen authenticating from the adversary's infrastructure in one coordinated action, remove the scheduled task and service, restore from a known-good backup predating first access, and monitor the restored host at elevated sensitivity for two weeks."}]},{"id":24,"title":"Digital Forensics Investigation","part":"Response","summary":"Recovering what happened from artefacts that were never meant to be a record.","sections":[{"h":"Order of volatility","p":"Collect from most volatile to least: memory and running state, network connections, temporary filesystem data, disk, then backups and archived logs. Every minute a compromised host stays running, memory evidence degrades - and every minute it stays running, the adversary may still be working."},{"h":"Windows execution artefacts","p":"Prefetch records that a binary ran and roughly when. ShimCache and AmCache record presence and path. UserAssist records interactive launches. Jump lists and shellbags record user navigation. Each is partial; together they build a defensible picture."},{"h":"Filesystem timestamps","p":"NTFS keeps two sets of timestamps - the standard information attribute, writable through the API, and the filename attribute in the master file table, which is not. When the two disagree, timestomping is the explanation, and the filename attribute usually preserves the real creation time."},{"h":"Log clearing","p":"Security event 1102 records the clearing of the security log and cannot itself be suppressed by the clearing. Clearing does not remove forwarded copies in the SIEM, nor the file-system and registry traces of the tool that did it. Clearing is an indicator, not an erasure."},{"h":"Memory","p":"Memory carries what disk does not: injected code with no file on disk, decrypted configuration, command history, credentials in process memory and network connections already closed. If a host is suspected of in-memory tooling, capture memory first or lose the case."},{"h":"Integrity","p":"Hash on acquisition, hash again after transfer, work only on copies, and record who held the evidence and when. Forensics without integrity controls produces interesting conclusions that cannot be used."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Browsing the live host with an administrator account and overwriting artefacts, trusting a single timestamp source, and rebooting to 'see if it comes back'."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"Prefetch shows a binary executed at 02:41. Its standard-information creation time claims a date three years earlier, but the master file table filename attribute shows the same day it ran. The stated age was a defence-evasion attempt, and the real execution window is now established."}]},{"id":25,"title":"Detection Engineering","part":"Engineering","summary":"Turning one investigation into a control that catches the next one.","sections":[{"h":"Purpose","p":"Every true positive should leave behind either a new detection or an improvement to an existing one. A SOC that investigates well but never engineers detections re-learns the same lesson at the same cost every quarter."},{"h":"What makes a good rule","p":"It targets behaviour rather than a replaceable artefact, it has a precise scope, it is documented with its intent and its expected false positives, and it is tested against historical data before it goes live."},{"h":"Backtesting","p":"Run the candidate logic over thirty to ninety days of historical data. Count the hits and inspect a sample. A rule that would have fired eleven thousand times is not a detection, it is a denial-of-service attack on your own analysts."},{"h":"Precision versus recall","p":"High recall catches everything and buries the team. High precision catches little and misses campaigns. Aim for detections whose true-positive rate justifies the analyst minutes they consume, and accept that a small number of low-precision, high-value rules on Tier-0 assets are worth the pain."},{"h":"Rule lifecycle","p":"Draft, backtest, tune, deploy in monitor-only, review, promote to alerting, then review on a schedule. Rules that no longer fire, or fire only on noise, are retired deliberately - not left to rot with their alerts silently routed to a folder nobody opens."},{"h":"Alert content","p":"The alert should carry what the analyst needs to start: host, user, process ancestry, source and destination, the matched field, the rule's intent and a link to the runbook. An alert that requires five pivots before triage can begin wastes the minutes that matter."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Writing rules from a blog post without backtesting, hardcoding indicators that expire in a week, alerting on parent-child relationships that the estate produces legitimately thousands of times a day, and never measuring whether a rule has ever produced a true positive."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"An investigation found an encoded PowerShell command spawned by a document process. A hash rule would age out instantly. The deployed rule instead alerts on an office application spawning a scripting engine with encoded arguments - behaviour the adversary must change tooling to avoid."}]},{"id":26,"title":"Writing SOC Reports","part":"Engineering","summary":"The written output is the only part of the work most people ever see.","sections":[{"h":"Audience","p":"One document usually has three audiences: executives who need impact and status, engineers who need specifics to act on, and the future analyst reading this during the next incident. Write the executive summary for the first, the body for the second, and keep the evidence references complete for the third."},{"h":"Structure that works","p":"Executive summary, initial detection, timeline, indicators, affected users and hosts, techniques and their mapping, evidence, root cause, impact, containment, eradication, recovery, detection improvements, and a conclusion that states confidence."},{"h":"Writing the timeline","p":"UTC everywhere, one line per event, each line tied to a source. Include the detection time and the actual first-activity time; the gap between them is often the most important number in the report."},{"h":"Facts, inference, and speculation","p":"Mark them apart. 'The account authenticated from 203.0.113.55 at 02:14 (source: security log 4624)' is a fact. 'The credentials were most likely obtained in the earlier phishing campaign' is an inference and must be labelled as one."},{"h":"Root cause","p":"Root cause is the control failure that allowed entry, not the last thing that went wrong. 'The user clicked a link' is rarely the root cause; 'remote access was reachable from the internet with single-factor authentication for a subset of accounts' usually is."},{"h":"Tone","p":"Neutral and specific. No blame on individuals, no dramatics, no hedging so heavy the reader cannot tell what you concluded. State the conclusion, state the confidence, state what would change it."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Pasting raw logs instead of summarising them, omitting what was not determined, writing recommendations nobody owns, and producing a report so long the executive summary is the only part read - while being the part written last and worst."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"A three-page report: half a page of executive summary with impact and status, a one-page timeline, half a page of indicators and affected assets, and a page of root cause, response actions and detection improvements with named owners and dates."}]},{"id":27,"title":"False Positives","part":"Engineering","summary":"Most of the queue is not an attack, and knowing why is a core skill.","sections":[{"h":"Why they dominate","p":"Detections are built to be sensitive, and enterprise environments do strange things legitimately. Administrators use the same remote-execution tooling as adversaries, backup software reads everything, and deployment systems push binaries to thousands of hosts at three in the morning."},{"h":"Classic sources","p":"Vulnerability scanners tripping every network rule; software deployment causing mass identical process creation; service accounts with stale passwords generating lockouts; disaster-recovery replication producing large scheduled outbound transfers; and support staff legitimately using debugging tools that touch process memory."},{"h":"How to prove it","p":"A false positive is proved, not assumed. Find the change record, the schedule, the owner's confirmation, the documented business process. 'It looks like a scanner' is not a closure; 'this matches CHG-4471, approved, same source, same window' is."},{"h":"The dangerous middle","p":"Adversaries deliberately hide in benign-looking activity: running during patch windows, using the deployment tool's own account, naming implants after legitimate services. Confirm the specifics - exact host, exact account, exact time window - rather than matching the shape of the activity."},{"h":"Tuning versus suppressing","p":"Tuning narrows a rule with a documented reason and a review date. Suppressing hides output. Suppression rules with no owner and no expiry are where intrusions are eventually found to have been visible all along."},{"h":"Measuring","p":"Track false-positive rate per rule. The worst handful of rules usually produce most of the noise, and fixing them returns more analyst time than any other single intervention."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Closing by pattern-matching to a previous closure, accepting a verbal 'that's normal' without a record, and tuning out an entire host class because one host was noisy."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"A large outbound transfer alerts at 02:00. The destination belongs to the contracted disaster-recovery provider, the volume matches the nightly baseline, and a standing change covers it. Closed as a false positive - with the evidence recorded, because the same alert on a different destination next week must not inherit that closure."}]},{"id":28,"title":"Real SOC Workflow","part":"Operations","summary":"How the day actually runs, from handover to handover.","sections":[{"h":"Shift structure","p":"Handover, queue review, triage, investigations, escalations, tuning and documentation, then handover again. Handover is the highest-risk moment of the day: partially worked investigations are where context is lost."},{"h":"Working the queue","p":"Rank by risk - asset criticality combined with detection severity and confidence - not by arrival order. Take ownership before investigating so two analysts do not duplicate work, and record the status so the queue tells the truth."},{"h":"Tiers in practice","p":"Tier 1 triages and either closes with evidence or escalates with context. Tier 2 investigates deeply and drives containment. Tier 3 hunts, does forensics and engineering. The boundaries move between organisations; what does not move is the requirement that an escalation carries the work already done."},{"h":"Escalating well","p":"A good escalation states what fired, what you checked, what you found, what you could not determine, and what you recommend. A bad escalation forwards the alert. The first buys the senior analyst twenty minutes; the second costs them twenty."},{"h":"Metrics that matter","p":"Time to detect, time to triage, time to contain, dwell time, escalation accuracy and false-positive rate per rule. Metrics that count closed alerts per analyst reward speed over correctness and will eventually close a real intrusion."},{"h":"Runbooks","p":"A runbook says what the alert means, what to check, what proves benign, what proves malicious, and when to escalate. Runbooks written after an incident, while the detail is fresh, are the ones that are actually used."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Working the queue top-down, leaving investigations unowned, never writing the note because the shift is busy, and treating the SIEM as the only tool when the answer is in the EDR."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"An analyst takes a domain-controller alert at handover, claims it, confirms the source is external and outside all change windows, writes four lines of findings, escalates to Tier 2 within the runbook's fifteen-minute window, and records the two queue items they closed as noise with their change references."}]},{"id":29,"title":"Career Preparation","part":"Operations","summary":"Getting into a SOC, and being worth keeping once you are there.","sections":[{"h":"What is actually assessed","p":"Interviews test reasoning under uncertainty far more than recall. Expect to be handed a log extract and asked what you see, what you would check next, and what would change your mind. Structured thinking beats memorised definitions every time."},{"h":"Foundations to build first","p":"Networking to the level of reading a flow record, Windows authentication and event logs, Linux logs and process model, Active Directory basics, scripting enough to parse and correlate, and the ability to write clearly. Tools are learned in weeks; these are not."},{"h":"A home lab","p":"A domain controller, a workstation, a Linux host, a log shipper and a free SIEM is enough to generate and investigate real telemetry. Detonating safe samples in an isolated lab and then hunting yourself teaches more than any number of walkthroughs."},{"h":"Certifications","p":"They open doors and structure study; they do not substitute for the ability to work a case. Blue-team practical certifications that require an investigation and a report are the ones that map closest to the job."},{"h":"Portfolio","p":"Write up investigations - lab or public data - in the report structure this book describes. A well-written incident report is more persuasive evidence of competence than a list of course names."},{"h":"The first ninety days","p":"Learn the environment before the tools: what is normal here, which hosts are critical, who owns what, which alerts are known noise and why. The analyst who knows the estate is the one whose judgement is trusted."},{"h":"Common mistakes","p":"Chasing tool names rather than fundamentals, avoiding writing, treating Tier 1 as a queue to escape rather than a craft, and burning out by taking every shift's worst alert personally."},{"h":"Practical example","p":"A candidate is shown fifteen authentication events and asked to comment. A weak answer names brute force immediately. A strong answer notes the account type, the source, the time relative to business hours, the success that followed the failures, and states what they would pull next - and why it might still be a stale service credential."}]},{"id":30,"title":"Advanced SOC Analyst Mindset","part":"Operations","summary":"How senior analysts think, and how everything in this book connects.","sections":[{"h":"Hypothesis before evidence","p":"Strong analysts state what they expect to see if a theory is true, then look. Weak analysts scroll until something catches the eye. The first method finds absence of evidence, which is itself informative; the second finds only what is visually striking."},{"h":"Disproving yourself","p":"Actively search for the evidence that would kill your conclusion. If a large transfer looks like exfiltration, look hard for the backup schedule. Confirmation bias is the single most expensive analytical failure in a SOC, and it produces confident, wrong reports."},{"h":"Baseline is everything","p":"Nothing is suspicious in isolation. Encoded PowerShell is normal in one estate and an incident in another. The analyst who knows what normal looks like on this network at this hour outperforms the analyst with better tools and no context."},{"h":"Thinking in chains","p":"Adversaries do not perform single actions. Access, execution, persistence, credentials, discovery, movement, collection, command and control, exfiltration, impact. When you find one link, the professional instinct is to ask what came before and what came after - not to close the one alert."},{"h":"Absence as evidence","p":"A missing log, a cleared event, a gap in beacon traffic, a host that stopped reporting - these are findings. Treat silence in telemetry as something to be explained rather than something to be scrolled past."},{"h":"Calibrated confidence","p":"Say low, moderate or high confidence, and say what would change it. Analysts who only ever speak in certainties lose credibility the first time they are wrong; analysts who never conclude anything are never asked again."},{"h":"Speed with rigour","p":"Containment is a race, but a wrong containment causes an outage and destroys evidence. Senior judgement is knowing which decisions are reversible and can be made fast, and which are not and must be made carefully."},{"h":"How this book connects","p":"Platforms give you the telemetry. Attack technique chapters tell you what to look for in it. Analysis chapters turn observations into assessments. Response chapters turn assessments into action. Engineering chapters turn action into controls, so the next intrusion is caught earlier. The loop - detect, investigate, respond, improve - is the whole job, and a SOC that runs it honestly gets measurably harder to breach every quarter."}]}];
